{
  "id": "server.models.list",
  "method": "GET",
  "path": "/api/models",
  "summary": "Every model the runner's settings name, where each stands, and what can be known of each without loading it. Its engine. Its `state`, the word a load or an unload of it answers (`model_state`): `up` - on the card and answering its health check - with its load; `loading` (its weights going up, or waiting for another load or for its stop to end); `waiting` for the card; `failed`, with why its last load failed, until a load of it begins again; or `off`. Whether it is `pinned`: kept by the open pipeline - named when it opened, or loaded with `/api/models/{key}/load` - and on the card until it is unloaded, the pipeline closes or is lost, or the server stops (health's `pipeline.models`); a model up and not pinned was loaded on demand, for a job, and comes off when it is idle. Its file (there or not, its size, the disk it sits on). Its file's `header`, read from the header alone, never the weights: a GGUF's for a llama.cpp model - a llama.cpp model's GGUF (`gguf_header`: the architecture, the context it was trained for, a chat template, bits per weight - said against the settings), a whisper model's ggml header (`shapes/ggml_header.json`: its eleven numbers, and its shape in words - `large v3`, `medium`, or its mels and layers); `context`, the context the settings ask of a llama.cpp model - null for a whisper model, which has none to set, the ggml header for a whisper model (`ggml_header`: its eleven numbers, in whisper.cpp's own order and under its own names, and its shape in words - `large v3`, `medium` - when it is one named, else its mels and its layers, never guessed at). Its load history from the bound folder's record, cold and warm (how many loads, the normal - the median, once there are three - the last, and whether the last was slow against the normal before it); and the last read-speed measure this server made. `slow_answer` is what this server judges a slow answer by (D3, D12) - null with no runner: an answer is far slower when it is at least `factor` times slower than its model's normal AND at least `floor_s` seconds lost over what its normal would have taken (the runner's settings, `slow_answer_factor` and `slow_answer_floor_s`), so a page shows the thresholds the server used, never ones typed into it. Its TIMING STATISTICS (`stats`, PLAN-4 D12), kept per model in the bound folder (`runner/model_stats.json`), each measure over all time - `count`, `mean`, `least`, `most`, `last` and when (`last_at`) - and of its last 200 samples (`kept`) the `median` and the 90th percentile (`p90`, a sample that happened): a load - its total from when it was asked for to ready (`load_s`), the wait for the card and the claim when that load took it (`load_wait_s`, `load_claim_s`), its steps - the process started, its port bound, healthy, its identity checked (`load_spawn_s`, `load_port_s`, `load_healthy_s`, `load_identity_s`; whisper-server's port, health and identity are one answer, `load_healthy_s`) - and the card's memory it took, at ready and after its first answer (`ready_mib`, `first_answer_mib`, where the card can be read); an answer - to its first token, whole (`first_token_s`, `answer_s`; in one piece, both the whole answer), its `tokens`, tokens a second once it began (`tokens_per_s`), prompt tokens a second to its first token (`prompt_tokens_per_s`), for audio its seconds and the real-time factor (`audio_s`, `real_time_factor`: seconds taken for each second of audio) - and, for each answer far slower than its model's normal, how many times its normal it took (`slow_answer`); an unload - from asking to its process gone, and to the card's memory back (`unload_s`, `unload_memory_s`); a job - the queue's own share of it, its time outside the model (`queue_s`). A measure nothing has measured yet is absent; `stats` is null with nothing bound, and when the kept summary cannot be read. `problems` are the sentences a load would log, a file that is not its engine's said in its reader's words. With nothing bound there is no history - `loads` and `loads_from` are null, never an empty record. With no runner behind the server there are no models.",
